Bound the audit log a guest can make the host write

Every refused connection is a line in the policy audit log, and the
guest decides how many there are. A program retrying a blocked address
in a loop -- or a compromised one doing it deliberately -- could fill
the host's disk from inside the sandbox: measured at 305 bytes a
refusal, with no cap and no rotation.

One file is capped and one generation is kept, so a sandbox holds at
most twice the limit however long a flood runs. Rotating rather than
refusing to write: the log is read to find out why something was
refused, and capping in place would freeze it at the moment the flood
started and hide everything after it.

